prompt:
Why do all my buttons alert 3?

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

text:
The cause is `var`. A `var` variable belongs to the whole function (or the global scope here), not to each pass of the loop. So there is only one `i`, and all three click handlers share it.

Here is what happens:

1. The loop runs and creates three buttons. Each handler holds a reference to the same `i`.
2. The loop ends when `i` reaches 3.
3. You click a button later, and the handler reads `i`, which is now 3.

The fix is to use `let` instead of `var`. A `let` variable in a `for` loop gets a fresh copy for each pass, so each handler keeps its own value:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.textContent = `Button ${i}`;
  b.onclick = () => alert(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

Now the buttons alert 0, 1, and 2. I also added `textContent` so the buttons show a label. Without it, they appear as empty, nearly invisible buttons.

If you can't use `let`, you can capture the value another way, such as with a function that takes `i` as an argument:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  const b = document.createElement('button');
  b.onclick = ((n) => () => alert(n))(i);
  document.body.append(b);
}
```

Here each call receives its own `n`, so the value is fixed at the moment the button is created.
